  "_comment": "DX number (part 1-part 2) as read on the film edge barcode -> film name and Belka profile. 'makers' names the assignee of each part 1 for numbers not listed in 'films'. The cartridge's six-digit code PHHHHE hashes the same number as HHHH = 16 * part1 + part2; most entries come from cartridge codes, which usually but not always match the edge code. 'uncertain': a single community report, or one number shared by several films; those never pick a profile. CineStill and other re-spooled motion-picture stocks carry KeyKode, not a DX edge code.",
  "_sources": {
    "i3a": "I3A, 'DX Codes for 135-Size Film' (2008), as transcribed in github.com/ffindr/dxcode-film-db, data/products.csv",
    "bfd": "The Big Film Database, thebigfilmdatabase.merinorus.com (search API, read 2026-10-02): cartridge codes and some edge readings",
    "flickr": "Flickr group 'DX barcode numbers on 135 film (please contribute)', flickr.com/groups/67377471@N00/discuss/72157634429783414",
    "wikipedia": "Wikipedia, 'DX number': assignees of part 1, from the I3A list of January 2009, and a photo of a BW400CN film edge read as 79-15",
    "belka": "decoded by Belka from the edge barcode of a real strip"
  },
